-- STILL REFUSED after M4: `MAYBE <enum>`.
--
-- M4 widens `MAYBE` to NUMBER and DATE (see ../maybe-scalars.l4) by pairing an
-- is-known boolean with a `show if: {code: …}`-guarded value question. That
-- design does not reach an enum payload, because the value question for an
-- enum is itself a `datatype: radio` whose absent path is not the widget's
-- empty submission but a fourth choice — a representation decision M4 does not
-- make. `MAYBE <record>` is refused for the same reason and worse: a record
-- explodes into several questions, and "the record is absent" would have to
-- gate all of them.
--
-- This fixture exists to keep the DIAGNOSTIC honest. At M1 the catch-all said
-- "v1: MAYBE BOOLEAN and MAYBE STRING only". The moment M4 lands NUMBER and
-- DATE that sentence is a false claim in user-facing prose, which is exactly
-- the drift the repo's CLAUDE.md warns about. The refusal must therefore name
-- what it is refusing — the enum — rather than recite a list that has moved on
-- without it.
--
-- The file itself typechecks: the refusal belongs to `l4 docassemble`.

DECLARE Severity IS ONE OF Critical, Reportable, Trivial

DECLARE Incident
    HAS
      @desc Triage class assigned to the breach, if one has been assigned yet
      `assessed severity` IS A MAYBE Severity

@export Whether the incident is still awaiting triage
GIVEN inc IS AN Incident @desc The incident under review
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the incident is awaiting triage` inc IF
    CONSIDER inc's `assessed severity`
    WHEN JUST s  THEN FALSE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE

#EVAL `the incident is awaiting triage` (Incident WITH `assessed severity` IS JUST Critical)  -- FALSE
#EVAL `the incident is awaiting triage` (Incident WITH `assessed severity` IS NOTHING)        -- TRUE
